<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Mac Catalyst ONLY. project.yml wires this in behind
	     CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS[sdk=macosx*], so no phone or simulator build
	     ever reads it and nutrition/nutrition.entitlements stays the iOS
	     answer, HealthKit and all.

	     It is EMPTY, and that is the entire point. The iOS file claims
	     com.apple.developer.healthkit, which is profile-backed: name it on a
	     macOS product and automatic signing has to mint a Mac Catalyst
	     development profile, which needs an Apple ID signed into Xcode. This
	     machine has none, so the build dies at GatherProvisioningInputs with
	     "No Accounts: Add a new account in Accounts settings." With nothing
	     profile-backed here, Xcode signs with the bare "Apple Development"
	     certificate and the build just works.

	     Nothing is lost. macOS has no Health app, isHealthDataAvailable() is
	     false, and HealthStore.authorizeHealthKit guards on it — so the Mac
	     uses the manually entered weight and body fat percentage, exactly as
	     a phone with those toggles off does.

	     It lives outside nutrition/ on purpose: the target's `sources:` sweep
	     would otherwise turn it into a stray file reference in the project,
	     and CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS resolves against SRCROOT and needs no
	     reference at all.

	     Deliberately NO com.apple.security.app-sandbox — project.yml carries
	     the argument for leaving the sandbox off. -->
</dict>
</plist>
